# Authentic URL FAinv three-compression analysis v2

## Result against the frozen prediction

The continuous Day0 trajectory reproduced the first compression exactly and reached three compressions after 70 examples:

| compression | trigger example | examples since prior trigger | accepted updates since prior | EM iterations | EM seconds |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 35 | 35 | 32 | 4 | 12.5557 |
| 2 | 51 | 16 | 16 | 4 | 11.5523 |
| 3 | 70 | 19 | 16 | 2 | 5.3973 |

Both post-first intervals were at most the preregistered 32-example threshold, and both later compressions required at least two EM iterations. The frozen dense-later-pattern prediction therefore passed, as did every validity and resource gate.

The implementation processed 64 accepted updates among 70 examples and made 13 prequential mistakes before stopping. Predictive variances remained finite and positive in `[8.2032, 99.6150]`; every materialized mean and EM output diagonal was finite, all EM loops crossed the released `1e-6` threshold, and the first trigger/iteration count matched v1. Peak RSS was 944,275,456 bytes and total wall time was 30.5393 seconds under the 4 GiB/180-second task limits.

## Runtime interpretation

The post-first trajectory accepted every update needed to refill the 16-column buffer, with only three rejected examples between the second and third triggers. This directly rejects the possibility that the first compression immediately creates a long quiet period. It also confirms that the deterministic one-iteration fixture was not representative of authentic early-stream geometry: the second real compression again required four iterations.

If the two post-first intervals and their mean EM time were stationary over the 36,000-example Day0-Day1 diagnostic, the local heuristic would be about 2,055 compressions and 17,417 EM seconds (about 4.84 hours), excluding prediction, parsing, Gram maintenance, lazy flushes and setup. This exceeds the 1,800-second command window by about 9.7 times. It is not a lower bound: only two post-first intervals were observed, later data can change the acceptance rate, and the published 20-day sampled protocol is different.

## Decision

Apply the prospective decision rule and retire `url-cw-fact-sparse-lazy-feasibility-v1` as a two-day URL application under the current CPU window. A larger URL run is not justified. The exact bounded lazy-mean identity and row-block compression remain valid implementation results, but they have strong prior-art overlap and no demonstrated quality or end-to-end resource advantage.

This retirement does not claim that the 2010 method fails statistically, that all sparse implementations are slow, or that the heuristic projection is globally stationary. It says the current source-guided rank-16 route is not a useful next experiment under the project's CPU constraints and strong-baseline discipline.

With the discriminative application branches now exhausted, the next serial direction should be a prior-art-and-protocol scope audit for a CPU-bounded generative-model use of streaming statistics. No generative experiment should start until it identifies a claim that is not already a standard streaming evaluation/sketching mechanism and names a strong exact/simple baseline.
